### 13. Context-sensitive home-screen variants

Do not use the same visual structure and CTA for every plant condition.

Create a reusable care presentation component supporting different engine-backed variants.

STABLE CONDITION:

Heading: Doing well.

Description: No watering needed right now.

Next check: Display the engine-supported check date when available.

Action: Optional soil check or care details, if relevant.

Do not manufacture an action merely to fill the interface.

WATERING REQUIRED:

Heading: Ready for water.

Quantity: Display the engine-supported volume or range.

Timing: Display the supported time window.

Action: Watering guide.

OVERWATERING RISK:

Heading: Hold off on watering.

Description: Explain the supported reason simply.

Action: Check soil or review drainage when recommended.

INSUFFICIENT INFORMATION:

Heading: Let's check your plant.

Description: Explain the missing information.

Action: Answer the relevant condition questions.

URGENT SUPPORTED CONDITION:

Heading: Needs attention.

Description: Concisely explain the supported urgency.

Action: Review urgent care.

ASSESSMENT UNAVAILABLE:

Heading: Condition unavailable.

Description: Explain that current care guidance could not be retrieved.

Action: Retry or view appropriately labeled previous information.

Never represent unavailable information as a healthy condition.

### 14. Progressive disclosure architecture

Implement three information levels.

LEVEL ONE: Immediate understanding.

Visible on Home.

What does my plant need?

What should I do?

When should I do it?

How much water, when relevant?

LEVEL TWO: Actionable guidance.

Accessible through the relevant primary action or a contextual sheet.

How should I perform the care action?

What should I check?

How much water is recommended?

Are there important precautions?

LEVEL THREE: Detailed intelligence.

Accessible through Care or the individual plant profile.

Soil composition.

Observation sources.

Drainage details.

Environmental parameters.

Watering history.

Assessment timestamps.

Engine rationale.

Uncertainty.

Species guidance.

Secondary conditions.

Do not present Level Three content as the default Home experience.

However, do not bury information necessary to prevent an inappropriate care action.

